(1. Basic Transmission Equations:) Tj
T*
(- Transmitted active power: P = sqrt\(3\) * V_L * I_L * cos\(Phi\)) Tj
T*
(- Line current: I_L = P / \(sqrt\(3\) * V_L * cos\(Phi\)\)) Tj
T*
(- Total three-phase line power loss: P_loss = 3 * I_L^2 * R_ph) Tj
T*
(- If R is the total loop resistance across the conductors, P_loss = I_L^2 * R.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Case \(a\): Transmission at V_1 = 66 kV \(66,000 V\):) Tj
T*
(- Line current: I_1 = \(500 * 10^6\) / \(sqrt\(3\) * 66,000 * 1.0\) = 4,373.86 A) Tj
T*
(- Total copper loss: P_loss1 = \(4,373.86\)^2 * 8 = 153,046,000 W = 153.05 MW) Tj
T*
(- Percentage loss = \(P_loss1 / P\) * 100 = \(153.05 / 500\) * 100 = 30.61%) Tj
T*
(- Observation: Over 30% of power is lost as heat; conductor would melt under thermal) Tj
T*
(stress!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Case \(b\): Transmission at V_2 = 400 kV \(400,000 V\):) Tj
T*
(- Line current: I_2 = \(500 * 10^6\) / \(sqrt\(3\) * 400,000 * 1.0\) = 721.69 A) Tj
T*
(- Total copper loss: P_loss2 = \(721.69\)^2 * 8 = 4,166,700 W = 4.17 MW) Tj
T*
(- Percentage loss = \(P_loss2 / P\) * 100 = \(4.17 / 500\) * 100 = 0.834%) Tj
T*
(- Observation: Stepping up to 400 kV slashes line losses from 30.6% down to just 0.83%!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. Case \(c\): Transmission at V_3 = 765 kV \(765,000 V\):) Tj
T*
(- Line current: I_3 = \(500 * 10^6\) / \(sqrt\(3\) * 765,000 * 1.0\) = 377.35 A) Tj
T*
(- Total copper loss: P_loss3 = \(377.35\)^2 * 8 = 1,139,150 W = 1.14 MW) Tj
T*
(- Percentage loss = \(1.14 / 500\) * 100 = 0.228%) Tj
T*
(- Loss reduction factor from 66 kV to 765 kV: \(765 / 66\)^2 = \(11.59\)^2 = 134.35 times) Tj
T*
(lower losses!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> Example 1.2: HVDC vs. HVAC Break-Even Economic & Technical Analysis) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Statement: A power planning commission evaluates transmitting 2,000 MW from an offshore) Tj
T*
(wind corridor over a distance of L km to a major industrial hub. The cost data is as) Tj
T*
(follows:) Tj
T*
(- HVAC Line Capital Cost: C_line_ac = Rs. 1.8 Crore per km) Tj
T*
(- HVAC Terminal Substations \(both ends\): C_term_ac = Rs. 400 Crore total) Tj
T*
(- HVDC Bipolar Line Capital Cost: C_line_dc = Rs. 1.1 Crore per km \(requires only 2) Tj
T*
(poles vs 3 phases\)) Tj
T*
(- HVDC Converter Stations \(both ends\): C_term_dc = Rs. 1,600 Crore total) Tj
T*
(\(a\) Calculate the break-even transmission distance \(L_break\) in kilometers.) Tj
T*
(\(b\) If the transmission route is 1,200 km, determine the total capital savings achieved) Tj
T*
(by selecting HVDC over HVAC.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Step-by-Step Solution:) Tj
T*
(1. Formulation of Total Capital Cost Equations:) Tj
T*
(- Total HVAC Cost: Cost_AC\(L\) = C_term_ac + \(C_line_ac * L\) = 400 + \(1.8 * L\) [in Rs.) Tj
T*
(Crore]) Tj
T*
(- Total HVDC Cost: Cost_DC\(L\) = C_term_dc + \(C_line_dc * L\) = 1,600 + \(1.1 * L\) [in Rs.) Tj
T*
