/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> Example 1.4: Grid Frequency Nadir and RoCoF Dynamics During Generator Outage) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Statement: A regional power grid has an aggregated operating capacity of S_n = 40 GW) Tj
T*
(\(40,000 MVA\) operating at 50.0 Hz. Suddenly, a major thermal power station generating) Tj
T*
(Delta_P = 1,200 MW trips offline.) Tj
T*
(\(a\) Calculate the initial Rate of Change of Frequency \(RoCoF = df/dt\) when the grid) Tj
T*
(inertia constant is H = 4.5 seconds \(conventional thermal/hydro grid\).) Tj
T*
(\(b\) Calculate the RoCoF if high penetration of inverter-based solar/wind resources) Tj
T*
(reduces effective grid inertia to H_eff = 2.0 seconds.) Tj
T*
(\(c\) Determine the critical response time for Fast Frequency Response \(FFR\) battery) Tj
T*
(energy storage systems \(BESS\) to prevent frequency from dropping below the) Tj
T*
(under-frequency load shedding \(UFLS\) trigger of 49.2 Hz.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Step-by-Step Solution:) Tj
T*
(1. Power System Swing Equation for Aggregated System Frequency:) Tj
T*
(- The fundamental relationship between power imbalance and frequency derivative is:) Tj
T*
(\(2 * H / f_0\) * \(df/dt\) = - Delta_P / S_n) Tj
T*
(- RoCoF: df/dt = - \(Delta_P * f_0\) / \(2 * H * S_n\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Case \(a\): Traditional High-Inertia Grid \(H = 4.5 s\):) Tj
T*
(- df/dt = - \(1,200 * 50.0\) / \(2 * 4.5 * 40,000\) = - \(60,000\) / \(360,000\) = - 0.1667 Hz/s) Tj
T*
(- Interpretation: Frequency drops at a manageable rate of 0.167 Hz per second, giving) Tj
T*
(thermal governors \(3-5 second response\) ample time to ramp up.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Case \(b\): Low-Inertia Renewable Grid \(H_eff = 2.0 s\):) Tj
T*
ET

